# /etc/default/pagespeed-optimizer -- host overrides for the
# pagespeed-optimizer daemon. Every value has a working default in the unit;
# uncomment to change.
#
# Secrets (PAGESPEED_API_TOKEN, PAGESPEED_PURGE_TOKEN) do NOT belong here.
# They live in /etc/pagespeed-optimizer/daemon.env (0640 root:pagespeed,
# read by systemd AFTER this file, so a value there overrides one set here)
# so they stay out of /proc/<pid>/cmdline. This file is pinned to 0640
# root:pagespeed as well -- the package re-asserts that on every install and
# upgrade (#1486) -- but it is configuration, not the secret store.

# Where the cache volume, shared config and serve-stats live. The daemon
# runs unprivileged as user `pagespeed` and must OWN the directory and
# everything in it; it refuses to start (loudly) on a missing, unwritable,
# or foreign-owned cache directory. Relocating it (to a dedicated cache
# mount, say) takes THREE steps: the unit's sandbox keeps the rest of the
# filesystem read-only, and its ReadWritePaths cannot name a directory that
# does not exist yet.
#   1. Create the directory first:  install -d -m 3770 -o pagespeed -g pagespeed /your/new/cache/dir
#   2. Extend the sandbox:          systemctl edit pagespeed-optimizer
#        [Service]
#        ReadWritePaths=/your/new/cache/dir
#   3. Set CACHE_DIR below, then:   systemctl restart pagespeed-optimizer
# Package purge removes only the default /var/cache/pagespeed-optimizer; a
# relocated cache directory is yours to remove. The old pre-2.1 location
# /var/lib/pagespeed-optimizer is never touched by the package: a
# pre-existing root-owned cache there is abandoned in place (cold start;
# no content is migrated or chowned).
# CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/pagespeed-optimizer/v1

# Notify/health/mgmt socket location. The default lives in the service's
# RuntimeDirectory (/run/pagespeed-optimizer, 0750 pagespeed:pagespeed);
# the serving module learns the real path from pagespeed-shared.conf, so a
# relocated socket needs no module-side change.
# SOCKET_PATH=/run/pagespeed-optimizer/notify.sock

# Cache volume size in bytes.
# CACHE_SIZE=536870912

# debug | info | warning | error
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Extra flags appended verbatim to the daemon command line. Never put
# secrets here -- see the note at the top.
#
# The HTTP management API is OFF by default and is enabled
# from here. The credentials it needs were generated into
# /etc/pagespeed-optimizer/daemon.env when the package installed, so
# enabling it is one line:
#
#   OPTIMIZER_OPTS=--api-socket
#
# That serves HTTP/1.1 over /run/pagespeed-optimizer/api.sock, mode 0660
# pagespeed:pagespeed. Membership in group `pagespeed` IS the credential for
# local peers: a connection accepted on that socket is an authenticated peer,
# so no bearer token is asked for -- nothing to provision to the web server,
# nothing to rotate -- and the daemon binds no TCP port at all, so "listens on
# localhost only" is something `ss -ltn` can prove rather than something you
# read off a config. That one line is the whole enablement; the generated
# token in daemon.env is there for the TCP case and is simply unused here.
#
# A TCP bind is still available for the deliberate remote case, and the
# daemon enforces one invariant at startup with a fatal, named refusal:
# REMOTE IS NEVER UNAUTHENTICATED, AND UNAUTHENTICATED IS NEVER REMOTE.
#
#   --api-port 9880                     loopback (127.0.0.1) by default;
#                                       mutually exclusive with --api-socket
#   --api-bind <addr>                   IPv4 literal only (no hostnames, no
#                                       IPv6); non-loopback needs BOTH a token
#                                       in daemon.env AND --api-allow-remote
#   --api-allow-remote                  confirm a deliberate off-host bind
#   --api-no-auth                       confirm a deliberate tokenless TCP
#                                       loopback API (the socket never needs
#                                       this -- its group scope is the auth)
#   --api-read-open                     open GET without a credential; this
#                                       is now a CHOICE, not something an
#                                       absent token does for you
#
# Publishing the API off-host? Put your reverse proxy's own authentication
# in front of it as well.
# OPTIMIZER_OPTS=

# Headless-Chrome sandbox: require | off.
#   require (default) -- if the kernel cannot give this service an
#     unprivileged user namespace, browser analysis REFUSES to start and says
#     so; the daemon keeps serving without it. It never falls back to an
#     unsandboxed browser.
#   off -- deliberate opt-out. --no-sandbox is passed, a WARNING banner is
#     logged on every browser start, and GET /v1/health reports
#     browser_sandbox: "off".
# There is no "auto": a mode that silently degrades is the defect this
# setting exists to remove. (Browser analysis is itself off by default.)
# PAGESPEED_BROWSER_SANDBOX=require

# Browser analysis on a host install. The package ships NO browser; it
# suggests one. Provide a Chromium/Chrome the `pagespeed` user can execute
# and point the daemon at it -- the compiled-in default path
# (/usr/bin/chrome-headless-shell) exists only in the container images:
#
#   Debian 12:                    apt-get install chromium
#                                 -> /usr/bin/chromium
#   Ubuntu 24.04: there is NO apt chromium -- `chromium-browser` is a stub
#                 that installs the snap, and a snap cannot run under this
#                 unit's sandbox. Use Google Chrome's .deb
#                                 -> /usr/bin/google-chrome-stable
#                 or pin Debian bookworm's chromium package (the recipe the
#                 product's container images use).
#   Google Chrome (.deb/.rpm), any distro:
#                                 -> /usr/bin/google-chrome-stable
#   EL9 (EPEL):                   dnf install chromium
#                                 -> /usr/bin/chromium-browser
#                                 (not exercised by the package's own
#                                 verification; the two above are)
#
#   OPTIMIZER_OPTS="--api-socket --enable-browser-analysis --chrome-binary /usr/bin/chromium"
#
# (Quote it: the value is split on whitespace into separate flags.) Then
# `systemctl restart pagespeed-optimizer` and read GET /v1/health over the
# API socket: browser_sandbox must be "on" and browser.chrome_running true.
# The shipped unit already runs Chrome sandboxed as-is -- PrivateUsers= is
# left unset and RestrictNamespaces= is not in the default unit for exactly
# this reason -- and the sandbox requires unprivileged user namespaces; on
# Ubuntu 24.04 and derivatives check
# kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns if the daemon reports the
# sandbox unavailable. Chrome writes its profile under the service's
# RuntimeDirectory (/run/pagespeed-optimizer), so no extra ReadWritePaths=
# is needed. With the enforcing syscall drop-in installed, install the 20-
# browser-analysis drop-in too (below).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Syscall filtering. NOT set from here -- and that is not
# an oversight.
#
# systemd resolves security directives when it LOADS the unit, before any
# EnvironmentFile= is read, and SystemCallFilter= does no variable
# expansion. A value in this file therefore cannot turn the filter on or
# off; a setting here that appeared to would be a lie. The switch is a
# drop-in.
#
# The shipped unit ENFORCES (since 2.1): the daemon may make the calls in
# systemd's @system-service group minus @privileged and @resources, and the
# kernel kills it with SIGSYS on the first call outside that set. The list
# is in the unit itself -- nothing to install to get it, and no drop-in that
# can be installed "alone" by mistake. SystemCallLog=~@system-service stays
# alongside it, so calls outside the group are still recorded.
#
# Browser analysis works under it with no extra step: Chrome's own sandbox
# needs a handful of calls the daemon's list does not admit, and the package
# ships that re-admission list INSTALLED AND ACTIVE as a vendor drop-in next
# to the unit (20-browser-analysis.conf under /usr/lib/systemd/system/
# pagespeed-optimizer.service.d/, /lib/systemd/... on Debian-family hosts).
# It only adds to the unit's allow-list. A host that does not run browser
# analysis can take the narrower daemon-only profile back by MASKING it --
# an empty file of the same name under /etc replaces the vendor one:
#
#   install -D -m 0644 /dev/null \
#     /etc/systemd/system/pagespeed-optimizer.service.d/20-browser-analysis.conf
#   systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart pagespeed-optimizer
#
# With it masked the daemon still starts and still serves; browser analysis
# refuses to initialize and names the restriction. It never falls back to an
# unsandboxed browser. Masking tightens; it is not the opt-out (below).
#
# OPT-OUT (back to the pre-2.1 log-only posture), surviving every package
# upgrade because the package never writes under /etc/systemd:
#
#   install -D -m 0644 \
#     /usr/share/doc/pagespeed-optimizer/90-syscall-filter-off.conf.example \
#     /etc/systemd/system/pagespeed-optimizer.service.d/90-syscall-filter-off.conf
#   systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart pagespeed-optimizer
#
# The file resets SystemCallFilter= and RestrictNamespaces=; the 90- prefix
# makes it sort after every vendor drop-in, which is what lets the reset
# win -- do not rename it. Remove it and repeat the two commands to go back
# to enforcing. Do not edit the unit under /usr/lib or /lib instead: an
# upgrade overwrites it.
#
# To confirm the posture, ask systemd -- not the daemon:
#
#   systemctl show -p SystemCallFilter pagespeed-optimizer
#     enforcing -> a long allow-list (no leading ~)
#     opted out -> SystemCallFilter=~   (an empty deny-list: nothing filtered)
#   systemd-analyze security pagespeed-optimizer
#
# The daemon's own "syscall_filter" field is NOT the confirmation: it reports
# "filtered" in both postures, because SystemCallLog= is itself a seccomp
# filter. It answers "is a filter attached", not "is this profile
# enforcing". (Reading it at all needs the management API enabled --
# OPTIMIZER_OPTS=--api-socket above; a stock install binds no API.)
#
# DIAGNOSING A KILL: `systemctl status` says `code=dumped, status=31/SYS`
# (daemon killed; no dump is written under LimitCORE=0), or the journal says
# `Chrome exited (status=0, signal=31)` (browser killed). The kernel's audit
# record (type 1326) names the call by NUMBER; where it lands depends on
# auditd, and an empty journal on a host that runs auditd means nothing:
#
#   systemctl is-active auditd
#     active   -> ausearch -m SECCOMP -ts recent -i   (-i names the call)
#                 (auditd owns the audit socket; the journal has NOTHING)
#     inactive -> journalctl -k | grep 'type=1326'    (the kernel ring; audit
#                 records carry no unit field, `journalctl -u` finds none)
#   a KILL reads sig=31 (code=0x8...); sig=0 code=0x7ffc0000 = log-filter record
#   ausyscall <N>                                     (number -> name)
#
# Report the name (with the Chrome version if the victim was the browser):
# a name missing from the browser profile is a bug in the shipped file, and
# the fix is a name in it, not a host that stays opted out.
#
# In a CONTAINER there is no systemd and none of the above applies: the
# container runtime's own profile is the filter, and /v1/health will say so.
# /usr/share/doc/pagespeed-optimizer/chrome-seccomp.json is the runtime
# profile to use (docker run --security-opt seccomp=...) when headless
# Chrome needs to build its own sandbox there.
